[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the two `%d` conversions and in the fact that the return value of `scanf` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **ignored**, so a failed conversion silently leaves the variable at its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m initializer of `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How this was verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/differential.rs` spawns both binaries as subprocesses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (never as a library), pipes identical bytes to stdin, and compares stdout,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr and exit status. The C binary is built by the test via CMake on first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m use if `c_src/build/driver` is absent. 25 tests, ~1,100 distinct inputs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m including a 400-case deterministic fuzz walk. Nothing is `#[ignore]`d.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Synthetic NUL byte pushed back after a bare sign at EOF
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status:** fixed in `src/main.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The original translation handled a sign followed by end-of-input via a helper:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn negative_advance(stream: &mut CStdin, c: &mut u8) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     *c = stream.getc().unwrap_or(0);   // EOF becomes a 0 byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The `0` sentinel then fell through to the non-digit branch and was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ungetc`'d, injecting a NUL byte that was never in the input into the stream
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for the *next* conversion to read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc does not do this. In `vfscanf`, `ungetc` is a macro that short-circuits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m on `EOF`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m #define ungetc(c, s) ((void) (c == EOF || (--read_in, _IO_sputbackc (s, c))))
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so after `scanf("%d")` consumes a sign and then hits EOF, nothing is pushed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m back and the stream stays at EOF.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The fix returns `None` directly on EOF-after-sign instead of manufacturing a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte, and folds the sign handling inline so the "one offending character is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pushed back, the sign is not" rule is explicit:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if c == b'-' || c == b'+' {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     negative = c == b'-';
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     match stream.getc() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         Some(b) => c = b,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         None => return None,   // glibc's ungetc is a no-op for EOF
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if !c.is_ascii_digit() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     stream.ungetc(c);          // only this character goes back
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     return None;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Observability:** with exactly two conversions and no further reads, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m divergence is not reachable from stdout — the injected NUL would itself be a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching failure for the second `%d`, leaving `y = 0`, which is what EOF
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produces too. Inputs `"-"`, `"+"`, `"-\n"`, `"5 -"` and `"--"` agree before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and after the change. It was fixed because the modeled stream semantics were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wrong, not because a test caught it; a third `scanf` would have exposed it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No other mismatch was found. Every other input class matched on the first run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors deliberately preserved (not "fixed")
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` crosses newlines.** `%d` skips all `isspace` characters, newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   included, so `"5\n\n\n3"` and `"5 3"` are identical inputs. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `scanf_reads_across_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Failed conversions leave `0`.** `scanf`'s return value is never checked,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so `"abc"` yields `x = y = 0` and prints `0 | ~0 == -1`. Empty input prints
